{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>My teaching shall drop as the rain, my word shall distil as the dew.</b> It appears to me along the lines of an interpretation I heard on the verse ״אָדָם וּבְהֵמָה תוֹשִׁיעַ ה׳״ (Psalm 36:7), according to the Jerusalem Talmud (Makkot 2:6): They asked of Prophecy, 'What shall a sinner do and attain atonement?' She answered: ״הַנֶּפֶשׁ הַחוֹטֵאת תָּמוּת״. They asked of the Torah, 'What shall a sinner do and attain atonement?' She answered: 'Let him bring an offering and attain atonement.' They asked of the Holy One, blessed be He, 'What shall a sinner do and attain atonement?' He answered: 'Let him do repentance and attain atonement,' as it says (Psalm 25:8): ״טוֹב וְיָשָׁר ה׳ עַל כֵּן יוֹרֶה חַטָּאִים בַּדָּרֶךְ״. And if so, from within the words of Prophecy there is no salvation for a sinful man, and from within the Torah's remedy there is no salvation for the beast, since in any case it must go to death to atone for the sin of the man. But from within the repentance that the Holy One, blessed be He, pronounced — let him do repentance and attain atonement — in this salvation is found for both of them, for man and beast. Therefore it says: ״אָדָם וּבְהֵמָה תוֹשִׁיעַ ה׳״." "And along this pattern these verses may be explained, according to the midrash brought in the Yalkut (Ha'azinu, remez 942): 'Look to the heavens — perhaps they changed their measure…'; ״וְתִשְׁמַע הָאָרֶץ״ — 'Look to the earth — perhaps she changed her measure…'. And the matter of the change of measure in man is his altering the will of his Creator and departing from the boundary of Torah and commandment. Therefore he said afterward that if you change your measures, then according to the view of Prophecy and the Torah it is impossible to escape slaughter, either for the man or for the beast. But the word of YHWH is purified to exempt man and beast from slaughter, and the sinner shall return to YHWH and He will heal him. This is what it says: ״יַעֲרֹף כַּמָּטָר לִקְחִי״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Ta'anit 7a): עֲרִיפָה means nothing but slaughter, as it says (Deuteronomy 21:4): ״וְעָרְפוּ שָׁם אֶת הָעֶגְלָה״, and ״לִקְחִי״ is a designation for Torah and for Prophecy, for Torah is called ״לֶקַח טוֹב״, and so too Prophecy is לְקוּחָה (received) from Him, blessed be He. And Moses said ״לִקְחִי״ — that is, what I myself received from HaShem: Torah and Prophecy, both of them shall be עָרוּף (slain), for from within both of them slaughter goes forth for one of them, either for the man or for the beast. And they are like this rain, in which not everyone rejoices — so too here there is never joy, either for the man or for the beast. But ״תִּזַּל כַּטַּל אִמְרָתִי״, for the word of Elohim, purified, brings joy to man and beast — like this dew, in which everyone rejoices. So too ״אָדָם וּבְהֵמָה תוֹשִׁיעַ ה׳״, and all rejoice in the salvation of HaShem, as it is written (Hosea 14:2–5–6): ״שׁוּבָה יִשְׂרָאֵל, אֶרְפָּא מְשׁוּבָתָם אוֹהֲבֵם נְדָבָה כִּי שָׁב אַפִּי מִמֶּנּוּ, אֶהְיֶה כַּטַּל לְיִשְׂרָאֵל יִפְרַח כַּשּׁוֹשַׁנָּה״ etc. And our Rabbis of blessed memory (Yoma 86b) learned from here that even if a single individual does repentance, he is forgiven together with the entire world, as it says: ״אֶרְפָּא מְשׁוּבָתָם״ — in plural language, and it is written: ״כִּי שָׁב אַפִּי מִמֶּנּוּ״." "And according to our words, it says ״אֶרְפָּא מְשׁוּבָתָם״, concerning both the man and the beast, as it says above (Hosea 14:3): ״וּנְשַׁלְּמָה פָרִים שְׂפָתֵינוּ״, for through the confession of our lips our debts are paid off, for through this the oxen too are freed from being offered as sacrifice, since the reason for the offering is that the bestial force within man — in it he is shared with the rest of the beasts and animals of the field. Therefore the beast comes as a substitute for the man, and in HaShem's compassion upon all His creatures, both man and beast, He indicated the way of repentance to the backsliding daughter. And even though the term מְשׁוּבָה applies only to the sinful man and not to the beast, nevertheless when a man brings an offering and leans his hand upon it and confesses over it, it is as though all his iniquities are borne by that beast, as it is written (Leviticus 16:22): ״וְנָשָׂא הַשָּׂעִיר עָלָיו אֶת כָּל עֲוֹנֹתָם״. Therefore it is fitting to include the beast as well in the term ״מְשׁוּבָתָם״. And it says ״אוֹהֲבֵם נְדָבָה״, for not by way of obligation do I love them, but rather willingly (בִּנְדָבָה), for thus did I create them at first willingly — so too now do I love all the work of My hands willingly. ״כִּי שָׁב אַפִּי מִמֶּנּוּ״ — from the man; therefore both are exempted, the man and the beast, for ״אֶהְיֶה כַּטַּל לְיִשְׂרָאֵל״ — like this dew in which all rejoice — ״יִפְרַח כַּשּׁוֹשַׁנָּה״, for every penitent is as though born anew on that day, as it is written (Psalm 103:3–5): ״הַסֹּלֵחַ לְכָל עֲוֹנֵכִי״, and adjacent to it: ״תִּתְחַדֵּשׁ כַּנֶּשֶׁר נְעוּרָיְכִי״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {2 "The Yalkut citation remez number (תתקמב = 942) and the Talmud tractate citations follow the Hebrew as given. The verse from Hosea is presented as a conjoined citation of verses 2, 5, and 6, as the Kli Yakar quotes them sequentially; this is preserved as he cites them."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:15:54.685511251Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}